{
  "title": "HAPI FHIR \u2014 healthcare (2,330\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: HAPI FHIR\nDomain: healthcare | Scale: large | Stars: 2,330\nProblem: Provides a comprehensive Java API for building HL7 FHIR-compliant healthcare clients and servers, enabling interoperability in health information exchange systems.\nTarget users: Healthcare software developers, Health IT system integrators, FHIR implementation teams, Electronic health record (EHR) vendors, Healthcare data exchange platforms\nCore features: FHIR Client API for consuming FHIR resources; FHIR Server framework for building compliant FHIR servers; FHIR resource parsing and serialization (JSON/XML); Data validation against FHIR specifications; RESTful FHIR API endpoint generation; Support for multiple FHIR versions (DSTU2, STU3, R4, R5)\nIntegrations: HL7 FHIR standard specifications, Maven Central Repository, Azure Pipelines CI/CD, CodeCov code coverage analysis\nTech stack: Java + Azure Pipelines\nKey libraries: HL7 FHIR libraries, RESTful framework components, XML/JSON serialization libraries\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Comprehensive healthcare standards implementation requires multi-version support for FHIR specifications, indicating evolution in domain requirements.\n- Strong CI/CD and code coverage practices critical for healthcare software; Azure Pipelines and CodeCov integration show emphasis on quality assurance.\n- Modular library design allows developers to use client, server, or resource components independently without heavy coupling.\n- Open-source healthcare infrastructure projects benefit from clear documentation, live demonstrations, and commercial support options (Smile CDR).",
